Step 2 — Create the detection rule
Now on Ubuntu:
sudo nano /var/ossec/etc/rules/local_rules.xml

Add:
<group name="local,authentication_failed,sshd,">

  <rule id="100100" level="7">
    <decoded_as>rhel-sshd-session</decoded_as>
    <description>RHEL SSH: Failed login using invalid user $(dstuser) from $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>

We're using rule ID 100100 because Wazuh recommends the 100000–120000 range for custom rules. Wazuh Documentation
Step 3 — Test the decoder BEFORE restarting anything
This is important.
Run:
sudo /var/ossec/bin/wazuh-logtest

Then paste your exact event:
Oct 5 22:16:09 localhost sshd-session[5647]: Failed password for invalid user wronguser from 192.168.95.129 port 55926 ssh2

We want Phase 2 to change from:
No decoder matched.

to something like:
name: 'rhel-sshd-session'
dstuser: 'wronguser'
srcip: '192.168.95.129'
srcport: '55926'

and Phase 3 should show:
id: '100100'
level: '7'
description: 'RHEL SSH: Failed login using invalid user...'
